# Recursion

**Recursion** is the definition of a thing through a smaller or earlier
instance of the same thing, ending at a base case. The factorial of $n$ is $n$
times the factorial of $n-1$, and the factorial of zero is one. Recursion is
the plainest form of mathematical self-reference and the standard anti-example
of a [[strange-loop]]: a definition that mentions itself, with nothing
referring to anything.

## Description

The discussion met recursion from two sides.

### As the first candidate, and the first to be ruled out

"There exists plenty of mathematical self-reference", the argument ran, and
the factorial function or any recursively defined sequence of numbers heads
the list. That is true and it is why recursion is the first class of the
[[taxonomy-of-self-reference]]: recursive generation, a rule repeated on
subproblems, with factorial, Fibonacci and fractals as its examples.

It is also where the list of anti-examples begins. A recursive definition
uses its own name, and that is all. The factorial function does not encode a
description of itself, says nothing about its own definition, and never
treats its definition as an object. Each call is the same kind of thing as
the one before, only smaller. There is no change of level, so there is
nothing for the descent to return *to*.

| | Recursion | Strange loop |
|---|---|---|
| Direction | Downward to smaller instances | Through levels and back to the start |
| Ends | At a base case | Does not end; it closes |
| Well-founded | Yes | No |
| Needs an encoding of itself | No | Yes |

The contrast is sharpest in what each one requires. Recursion requires a
base case to stand on. A strange loop has no bottom: the Gödel sentence refers
to its own code, not to a smaller sentence.

### As one of the mechanical constructions

The second approach came from asking what is "even before recursion".
Mathematics is full of step-by-step connections: position, velocity and
acceleration as first and second derivatives; addition lifted to
multiplication and multiplication to exponentiation. These are operations
applied, repeated and lifted. Recursion belongs with them, in the layer of
operations, and not with the reflective phenomena of self-description and
diagonalization, which sit two layers higher in the organisation proposed
there.

That placement gives recursion its proper partner. [[mathematical-induction]]
is its twin on the side of proof: recursion defines the object for $n+1$ from
the object for $n$, induction proves the property for $n+1$ from the property
for $n$. Along the ordinals the same pair builds the
[[constructible-universe]], which is why that construction, for all its
self-description, is well-founded and not a loop in time.

### Recursion that is not an anti-example

Recursion can be one ingredient of real self-reference without supplying it.
Gödel's construction uses recursive definitions throughout, to define
formulas, proofs and substitution, and the self-reference comes from the
encoding and the diagonal step, not from the recursion. A [[fixed-point]]
combinator obtains recursion from self-application, which is the reverse
direction: self-reference producing recursion. The summary given in the
discussion holds for both: recursion does not suffice.

Recursion is also distinct from a [[feedback-loop]]. Recursion is a
definition, outside time, descending and terminating. Feedback is a causal
cycle in time that may run indefinitely.

## Relations

- It is not a [[strange-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}: it descends to a base case and crosses no level.
- It is the weakest form of [[self-reference]] {relation: instance-of}: a definition that mentions itself.
- It is the first class, recursive generation, of the [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]] {relation: instance-of}.
- Its proof-side twin is [[mathematical-induction]] {relation: analogous-to}.
- It is not a [[feedback-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}: a definition through smaller instances, against a causal cycle in time.
- It is the usual source of [[self-similarity]] {relation: generalizes}: a rule repeated on its own output.
- Its transfinite form builds the [[constructible-universe]] {relation: uses} stage by stage.
- It can be obtained from a [[fixed-point]] {relation: uses} of a functional, as the Y combinator does.
- It is used by, without being the source of self-reference in, [[godel-numbering]] {relation: uses}.
- Its repeated-step relatives are [[iteration]] {relation: analogous-to} and the [[hyperoperation]] {relation: example-of} hierarchy.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[mathematical-induction]].** The proof scheme of base case and step. It
  proves things about exactly the objects that recursion defines, and shares
  recursion's well-foundedness.
- **[[self-similarity]].** Resemblance of a structure to itself across
  scales. It is what recursion looks like when drawn, and equally short of
  self-reference.
- **[[strange-loop]].** A return to the starting level through a crossing of
  levels of description. Recursion has the repetition and lacks both the
  crossing and the return.
- **[[taxonomy-of-self-reference]].** Eight classes of self-reference, from
  recursive generation to self-modelling cognition. Recursion is the first
  and least reflective.
- **[[fixed-point]].** A value that a transformation maps to itself.
  Fixed-point constructions show recursion arising from self-application.

